prompt:
Create a DataTexture-based, GPU-accelerated Boids flocking algorithm simulation using Three.js, with 500 particles following separation-alignment-cohesion rules.

[Three.js Import Method (Must Be Followed Strictly)]
Use the following importmap in <head>, and import via ES Modules in <script type="module">:
<script type="importmap">
{ "imports": {
    "three": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/build/three.module.js",
    "three/addons/": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.170.0/examples/jsm/"
} }
</script>
<script type="module">
import * as THREE from 'three';
import { OrbitControls } from 'three/addons/controls/OrbitControls.js';
import { GPUComputationRenderer } from 'three/addons/misc/GPUComputationRenderer.js';
// Write your code here
</script>

[GPGPU Architecture]
(1) Use GPUComputationRenderer (from three/addons/misc/GPUComputationRenderer.js) to implement the Boids simulation on the GPU;
(2) Two DataTextures (dimensions width=32, height=16 → 512 texels, with the first 500 texels storing boid data):
  - texturePosition: RGBA → (x, y, z, unused or stores heading)
  - textureVelocity: RGBA → (vx, vy, vz, unused or stores speed)
(3) Position initialization: randomly distribute 500 boids within the [-25, 25]³ cube;
(4) Velocity initialization: random directions, with speeds of 1-3 units/s;

[Boids Rules—GPU Shaders]
(5) Implement the three classic Boids rules in the velocity compute shader:
  - Separation: maintain distance from neighbors within separationRadius, producing a repulsive force;
  - Alignment: align with the velocity directions of neighbors within alignmentRadius;
  - Cohesion: move toward the centroid of neighbors within alignmentRadius, with strength controlled by the cohesion parameter;
(6) Compose the new velocity = old velocity + separation * separationWeight + alignment * alignmentWeight + cohesion * cohesionWeight;
(7) Speed limit: normalize and multiply by speedLimit if the speed exceeds speedLimit;

[Boundary Handling]
(8) Boids move within the [-25, 25]³ cubic space;
(9) When a boid approaches a boundary (distance < 5 units), apply a restoring force toward the center proportional to the amount of boundary encroachment;

[Predator Effect]
(10) When the mouse moves over the viewport, use a Raycaster to project the mouse position onto the y=0 plane to obtain a 3D position;
(11) Pass this position to the compute shader as the predator uniform;
(12) When a boid is less than 10 units from the predator, generate an escape force directed away from the predator, with strength inversely proportional to the distance;

[Rendering]
(13) Render 500 boids using InstancedMesh (use ConeGeometry as the shape of an individual boid, height=0.5, radius=0.15);
(14) Read data back from the position/velocity DataTextures every frame (using renderer.readRenderTargetPixels or reading from texture.image.data on the JS side), and update each instance matrix:
  - Obtain position from texturePosition
  - Obtain orientation from textureVelocity (the cone tip points in the velocity direction, using quaternion.setFromUnitVectors)
(15) Boid color: map color temperature according to speed magnitude (slow=blue, fast=red), or use a uniform bright color;

[Visualization Aids]
(16) Cube bounding box: use LineSegments to draw the wireframe cube for [-25, 25]³ (white and semi-transparent);
(17) Optional: boid trails or motion blur (enhances the visual effect but is not required);

[Control Panel]
(18) Separation radius slider id="separationRadius", range 1-10, step 0.5, default 3;
(19) Alignment radius slider id="alignmentRadius", range 5-20, step 1, default 10;
(20) Cohesion strength slider id="cohesion", range 0-2, step 0.1, default 1.0;
(21) Speed limit slider id="speedLimit", range 1-20, step 1, default 8;

[State Exposure (Required)]
(22) Update window.__3D_STATE__ every frame (or every 10 frames), containing:
  - boidCount: number (500)
  - separationRadius: number
  - alignmentRadius: number
  - cohesion: number
  - speedLimit: number
  - avgSpeed: number (the average scalar speed of all boids)
  - avgNeighborDist: number (the average distance of 50 randomly sampled boid pairs, or another reasonable aggregation metric)
  - centerOfMass: { x: number, y: number, z: number } (the centroid coordinates of the 500 boids)

Output a single HTML file that can run when opened directly in a browser.
